diff --git a/src/engine/backup/mod.rs b/src/engine/backup/mod.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..53458dd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/engine/backup/mod.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,328 @@
+//! A backup of a store: the directory an operator moves, and the manifest that says what moved.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.19-backup-restore`. Everything before this step treats the store as a *place a
+//! process writes to*: `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery` replays its log, `lakelet/4.8-compaction` names
+//! its segments, `lakelet/4.16-profiling` measures what writing them costs. This is the first step
+//! that treats the store as **a unit somebody else moves** — and the two numbers that decide whether
+//! moving it was worth anything are RPO (what the crash had already lost when the copy began) and
+//! RTO (how long putting it back takes). `docs/dr-drill.md` is the page those two numbers are
+//! written on, and [`copy`] and [`restore`] are what it measures.
+//!
+//! # What a copy of this store is
+//!
+//! Three things, and the order they are read in is the order they are written in:
+//!
+//! 1. **the store's own manifest** (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s `segments.manifest`) — read *first*,
+//!    because it is the file list. The manifest is the store: a file in `segments/` the manifest does
+//!    not name is an orphan a crash left behind, and an orphan is not part of what a store is, so it
+//!    is not part of a copy of one either. Reading it through the store's own reader is also what
+//!    checks every segment it names against its file, so a manifest naming a file nobody kept is a
+//!    refusal here rather than a copy with a hole in it;
+//! 2. **every segment the manifest names**, in the manifest's order — [`file_of`](compaction::file_of)
+//!    is the only place a segment's path is decided, and the order is the order the records were
+//!    accepted in;
+//! 3. **the log's proven prefix** — `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s replay, and exactly the bytes it
+//!    proves. This is where a copy differs from `cp -r`: the tail no replay can prove is *not* part
+//!    of what the store is (it is the middle of a record nobody finished), and copying it would put
+//!    bytes nobody can read into a backup and into every store restored from it.
+//!
+//! Two things are deliberately **not** copied. The sidecar indexes (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`) are
+//! derived state: a store without one answers the same questions having read more, and a restore that
+//! rebuilt them by answering more questions would be a restore doing work the store can do itself.
+//! And `*.pending` is a half-written manifest that `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s own open deletes: a
+//! copy that took one would be copying a file the store has already decided not to keep.
+//!
+//! # Why the copy's manifest is a log
+//!
+//! `<copy>/backup.manifest` is `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s format, byte for byte — see
+//! [`manifest`] for the layout and the argument. The half of it that matters here is the ordering: a
+//! copy writes a file's bytes, waits for the device, and only then appends the entry naming it. So
+//! "the manifest says what is complete" is a property of the code rather than a claim about the
+//! filesystem: an entry is there only after the bytes it describes were on the device, and a copy
+//! interrupted anywhere leaves a manifest naming exactly the files that survived it.
+//!
+//! That is also what makes the interruption a *value* ([`Interruption`]). Nothing here reads a
+//! clock, installs a signal handler or starts a thread: a caller that wants to know what a copy
+//! interrupted at a particular byte leaves asks for [`Interruption::AfterBytes`] and gets a copy that
+//! stopped there. The death is injected, so the drill in `tests/sim_backup.rs` can put it at a
+//! seeded point and the page can quote what came out.
+//!
+//! # What a copy of a *live* store would need, and what this one does not do
+//!
+//! A store being written while it is copied is a store whose files agree with each other only by
+//! accident: the copy reads the manifest, then each segment, then the log, and a flush or a merge
+//! between two of those reads leaves a copy of two different stores. This step's answer is an
+//! operator's (`docs/dr-drill.md`: stop the writer, copy, verify, restore into a place that is not
+//! the store, and swap), and the file below is what makes it possible. Doing it live needs a
+//! *checkpoint* the store can hand out — a manifest, a set of segments and a log offset that belong
+//! together, plus the store's agreement not to retire the segments a copy in flight named
+//! (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s snapshots are that promise for a reader, and this step deliberately
+//! does not invent a second one).
+//!
+//! # What this module does not do
+//!
+//! It does not parse a segment, and it does not re-encode anything: a copy is a copy of the bytes
+//! the store wrote, and a restore is those bytes written somewhere else. The restored store is a
+//! store like any other — `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s open is what reads it, `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`
+//! is what rebuilds its ledger — which is the whole reason a restore needs no code of its own.
+
+pub mod manifest;
+pub mod restore;
+
+pub use manifest::{Entry, Kind, Loaded, Manifest};
+pub use restore::{Damage, Outcome, restore};
+
+use std::fmt;
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+
+use crate::error::Error as StoreError;
+
+/// How far a copy is allowed to get: the death this step is about, as a value.
+///
+/// `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr` turned "when does the store wait for the device" into a value a
+/// caller can point at, and this is the same habit one level up: a machine that stops while a copy is
+/// running is not something a test can ask for by waiting, and a copy that takes a *decision* about
+/// where it stopped can be asked for exactly. No signal, no clock and no thread is involved — the
+/// caller decides, and the copy obeys.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Interruption {
+    /// Copy everything.
+    None,
+    /// Stop once this many bytes of file *contents* have been written.
+    ///
+    /// A file whose bytes the budget cuts through is left partial and is **not** named — the manifest
+    /// is what says a file is complete — and a file the budget ends exactly on is written whole and
+    /// named, with the file after it not started. `AfterBytes(0)` therefore writes nothing at all.
+    AfterBytes(u64),
+}
+
+impl Interruption {
+    /// How many bytes of file contents this interruption allows, or `None` for all of them.
+    #[must_use]
+    const fn budget(self) -> Option<u64> {
+        match self {
+            Self::None => None,
+            Self::AfterBytes(bytes) => Some(bytes),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Why a copy or a restore did not happen.
+///
+/// One type for both, because the two commands answer one question each and a script reads their
+/// classes the same way (`lakelet/3.15-cli-contract`'s table): the *class* is what a caller branches
+/// on, and [`Error::class`] is the one place that decides it — the command line does not keep a
+/// second table of what a refusal exits with.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+pub enum Error {
+    /// There is no manifest at this path: no store to copy, or no copy to restore.
+    NoManifest {
+        /// The manifest that is not there.
+        path: PathBuf,
+    },
+    /// The store a copy was asked about refused to be read: its own typed error, unaltered.
+    ///
+    /// A manifest that is not this build's, a segment that is not the length the manifest states, a
+    /// log that is not this store's — all of them are `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s and
+    /// `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s refusals, and a copy that re-worded them would be a copy an
+    /// operator cannot compare with what the store says about itself.
+    Source {
+        /// What the store's own reader refused with.
+        source: StoreError,
+    },
+    /// A copy is not what its own manifest says it is.
+    Damaged(Damage),
+    /// The destination already holds a store.
+    ///
+    /// A store is the directory whose `segments.manifest` is there (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`), so
+    /// this is the check that makes "a restore never overwrites and never merges" a property of the
+    /// code rather than a hope — and the same check is what stops a copy from being written over a
+    /// store, which would lose the store it copied. A copy's own directory holds that file too (it is
+    /// one of the things a copy takes), which is why [`copy`] reads it beside the copy's own manifest
+    /// before calling a destination a store.
+    Occupied {
+        /// The `segments.manifest` that is already there.
+        path: PathBuf,
+    },
+    /// The filesystem refused.
+    Store {
+        /// The file or directory it refused about.
+        path: PathBuf,
+        /// What the operating system said.
+        source: std::io::Error,
+    },
+}
+
+impl Error {
+    /// The exit code the command line leaves behind for this failure.
+    ///
+    /// The numbers are `lakelet/3.15-cli-contract`'s table: `3` when the thing asked about is not
+    /// there (no store to copy, no copy to restore — the state an operator most often meets is a
+    /// path that is wrong, and that is not a refusal), and `4` when the store or the copy refused
+    /// (a damaged copy, a destination that already holds a store, a filesystem that would not). `0`,
+    /// `1` and `2` are not here: a failure is never an answer, whether this *deployment* is broken
+    /// is a question about the process rather than about the store, and a wrong call is answered
+    /// before a store is opened at all.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn class(&self) -> u8 {
+        match self {
+            Self::NoManifest { .. } => 3,
+            Self::Source { .. } | Self::Damaged(_) | Self::Occupied { .. } | Self::Store { .. } => {
+                4
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Error {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        match self {
+            Self::NoManifest { path } => write!(
+                formatter,
+                "there is no manifest at {}: a store holds `segments.manifest`, a copy holds \
+                 `backup.manifest`, and neither is here",
+                path.display()
+            ),
+            Self::Source { source } => write!(formatter, "{source}"),
+            Self::Damaged(damage) => write!(formatter, "{damage}"),
+            Self::Occupied { path } => write!(
+                formatter,
+                "{} is a store's own manifest and it is already there: a restore never overwrites \
+                 and never merges, and a copy is not written over a store",
+                path.display()
+            ),
+            Self::Store { path, source } => {
+                write!(
+                    formatter,
+                    "the filesystem refused {}: {source}",
+                    path.display()
+                )
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+impl std::error::Error for Error {
+    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {
+        match self {
+            Self::Source { source } => Some(source),
+            Self::Store { source, .. } => Some(source),
+            Self::NoManifest { .. } | Self::Damaged(_) | Self::Occupied { .. } => None,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one copy did: the files it took, what they cost, and what the store was.
+///
+/// A value with accessors and no `Display`: the shape is the command line's (`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`
+/// made `name=value` lines the answer of a tool, and `--json` the same object), and a report that
+/// printed itself would be a second place that decides what an operator reads.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Report {
+    /// Files the copy wrote anything for, the file an interruption left partial included.
+    files: u64,
+    /// Entries the copy's own manifest holds: the files it wrote *whole*.
+    entries: u64,
+    /// Bytes of file contents the copy wrote.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// Records the segments it named hold, from the store's own index.
+    records: u64,
+    /// Bytes of the log's proven prefix it took.
+    log_bytes: u64,
+    /// Records that prefix holds.
+    log_records: u64,
+    /// `true` when the copy stopped before the store was done.
+    interrupted: bool,
+    /// How many times the store it copied had changed shape.
+    epoch: u64,
+    /// The millisecond that store had forgotten up to.
+    floor: u64,
+}
+
+impl Report {
+    /// Files the copy wrote anything for: everything it named, plus the file an interruption cut
+    /// through (which nothing names).
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn files(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.files
+    }
+
+    /// Entries the copy's manifest holds, which is the number of files it wrote whole.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn entries(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.entries
+    }
+
+    /// Bytes of file contents the copy wrote.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes
+    }
+
+    /// Records the segments it named hold, from the store's own index.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records
+    }
+
+    /// Bytes of the log's proven prefix it took.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn log_bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.log_bytes
+    }
+
+    /// Records that prefix holds.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn log_records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.log_records
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when the copy stopped before the store was done.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn interrupted(&self) -> bool {
+        self.interrupted
+    }
+
+    /// The epoch of the store the copy was taken of.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn epoch(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.epoch
+    }
+
+    /// The millisecond that store had forgotten up to when it was copied.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn floor(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.floor
+    }
+}
+
+/// Copy the store at `source` into `destination`.
+///
+/// The order is the module docs': the store's own manifest, every segment it names in its order, and
+/// the log's proven prefix. Each file is written, flushed and only then named in the copy's own
+/// manifest, so what a copy of an interrupted run declares is exactly what survived it.
+///
+/// The source is read, never written through: the files this call takes are the ones the store's own
+/// reader names, and nothing here changes a byte of them. `destination` is created if it is not
+/// there, and its own `backup.manifest` is truncated when the copy starts — the entries of a run are
+/// the files that run wrote.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::NoManifest`] when `source` holds no store, [`Error::Occupied`] when `destination`
+/// already holds one, [`Error::Source`] when the store refuses to be read (a segment its manifest
+/// names is missing or is not the length it states, its manifest or its log is not this build's), and
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the filesystem refuses.
+pub fn copy(
+    source: &Path,
+    destination: &Path,
+    interruption: Interruption,
+) -> Result<Report, Error> {
+    let _ = (source, destination, interruption);
+    todo!(
+        "lakelet/4.19-backup-restore: the store's own manifest first (it is the file list), then \
+         every segment it names in its order, then the log's proven prefix — and per file: write the \
+         bytes, wait for the device, and only then append the entry that names it. Stop where \
+         `interruption` says, leaving the file it cut through partial and unnamed."
+    )
+}
diff --git a/src/engine/backup/restore.rs b/src/engine/backup/restore.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c35ace5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/engine/backup/restore.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,207 @@
+//! Putting a copy back: what the copy says, and what this module refuses to believe.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.19-backup-restore`. A copy is a directory, a manifest and a pile of files, and
+//! none of the three is evidence on its own. **A backup nobody has verified is not a backup**: a
+//! copy of a store whose segment lost a byte in the filesystem, whose transfer was cut short, or
+//! whose disk wrote something else is a directory that looks exactly like a good one until somebody
+//! restores it — and the moment an operator finds out is the moment they needed it. So this module
+//! is the only place that judgement is made, and it makes it in one order:
+//!
+//! 1. **read the copy's manifest** ([`Manifest::load`](super::Manifest::load)) — which is what says
+//!    what the copy *claims*: the file list, each file's length, and the crc32 over its bytes;
+//! 2. **verify every entry before writing anything** — the file is there, it is the length the
+//!    manifest states, and its bytes hash to the checksum the manifest states. The whole pass happens
+//!    before the first byte of the destination is written, so a refused restore leaves the
+//!    destination exactly as it found it: no half-restored directory for an operator to open, and
+//!    nothing to clean up before trying again;
+//! 3. **write the files, and the store's own manifest last.** `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s rule is that
+//!    the manifest is the store — it is the file a directory becomes a store by having — so the last
+//!    write is the commit point, and a restore interrupted before it leaves a directory that no store
+//!    will open as a store. That sentence matters to an operator for a reason that is not obvious:
+//!    opening such a directory is *destructive*, because a store deletes what its manifest does not
+//!    name and a directory with segments and no manifest is a store with orphans. A half-restore is
+//!    not a store; do not open one to look.
+//!
+//! Nothing here parses a segment and nothing here re-encodes anything. A restore is a file copy, and
+//! the store that comes out of it is opened the way any other store is
+//! (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s open replays what it needs, `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`'s ledger
+//! is rebuilt from it) — which is why a restore can be this short: there is no second store format
+//! for it to be a reader of.
+//!
+//! # What the numbers are for
+//!
+//! [`Outcome`] reports what was written, not what could have been: how many files, how many bytes,
+//! how many records they hold. The two numbers an operator actually decides on are RPO, which is
+//! *before* this module — what the crash had already lost when the copy was taken, which is the write
+//! path's policy and not a restore's business (`lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr`) — and RTO, which is how
+//! long this call took. `docs/dr-drill.md` is where both are written down with the machine they were
+//! measured on.
+
+use std::fmt;
+use std::path::Path;
+
+use super::{Entry, Error};
+
+/// Why a copy is not what its own manifest says it is.
+///
+/// Four answers, and they are four because an operator does something different about each: a file
+/// that is **missing** means the copy never completed or lost a piece of it on the way, one that is
+/// **short** means the bytes that arrived are not all of them, one whose **checksum** does not match
+/// means the bytes are the wrong bytes, and one that cannot be **read** at all is a filesystem or a
+/// permission problem rather than a copy problem.
+///
+/// Every sentence names the file, and every sentence that has two numbers states both — an operator
+/// who is told "the copy is damaged" has been told nothing they can act on, and the pair of numbers
+/// is what says whether it is one byte or one file.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Damage {
+    /// The manifest names a file the copy does not have.
+    Missing {
+        /// The file, as the entry names it.
+        name: String,
+    },
+    /// The file is not the length the manifest states.
+    Short {
+        /// The file, as the entry names it.
+        name: String,
+        /// How many bytes the manifest states.
+        expected: u64,
+        /// How many the file holds.
+        found: u64,
+    },
+    /// The file's bytes do not hash to the checksum the manifest states.
+    Checksum {
+        /// The file, as the entry names it.
+        name: String,
+        /// The crc32 the manifest states.
+        expected: u32,
+        /// The crc32 the file's bytes compute.
+        found: u32,
+    },
+    /// The file cannot be read at all, or its name is not one the store could have written.
+    Unreadable {
+        /// The file, as the entry names it.
+        name: String,
+        /// What the filesystem said, or which part of the name is not one a copy holds.
+        detail: String,
+    },
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Damage {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        match self {
+            Self::Missing { name } => write!(
+                formatter,
+                "{name}: the copy's manifest names it and the copy does not hold it"
+            ),
+            Self::Short {
+                name,
+                expected,
+                found,
+            } => write!(
+                formatter,
+                "{name}: {expected} bytes expected, {found} found"
+            ),
+            Self::Checksum {
+                name,
+                expected,
+                found,
+            } => write!(
+                formatter,
+                "{name}: crc32 {expected:08x} expected, {found:08x} found"
+            ),
+            Self::Unreadable { name, detail } => write!(formatter, "{name}: {detail}"),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one restore wrote.
+///
+/// The counts are of the destination and not of the copy: `files` is what was written, `bytes` is
+/// what those files cost, and `records` is what a restored store will answer with, from the entries
+/// the copy's manifest stated counts for. The epoch and the floor are the store's own two numbers,
+/// carried by the copy (`super::manifest` says where they live) so that a restore can state which
+/// store it put back without opening the directory it just wrote — which matters, because a restore
+/// of an *interrupted* copy produces a directory no store opens, and an operator still needs to be
+/// told what it was a copy of.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Outcome {
+    /// Files written into the destination.
+    files: u64,
+    /// Bytes those files cost together.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// Records the segments written hold, from the copy's own entries.
+    records: u64,
+    /// Records the log's proven prefix holds.
+    log_records: u64,
+    /// The epoch of the store this copy was taken of.
+    epoch: u64,
+    /// The millisecond that store had forgotten up to.
+    floor: u64,
+}
+
+impl Outcome {
+    /// Files written into the destination.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn files(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.files
+    }
+
+    /// Bytes those files cost together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes
+    }
+
+    /// Records the segments written hold, from the copy's own entries.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records
+    }
+
+    /// Records the log's proven prefix holds.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn log_records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.log_records
+    }
+
+    /// The epoch of the store this copy was taken of.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn epoch(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.epoch
+    }
+
+    /// The millisecond that store had forgotten up to.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn floor(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.floor
+    }
+}
+
+/// Put the copy at `source` back into `destination`.
+///
+/// The order is the module docs', and it is the whole of the design: load the copy's manifest, verify
+/// every entry it names, create the destination, write everything but the store's own manifest, and
+/// write that last. A refusal anywhere in the first two steps leaves the destination untouched.
+///
+/// `destination` may be created by this call, and it may be a directory with other files in it: what
+/// this refuses is a destination that already holds a *store* (a `segments.manifest`), because a
+/// restore never overwrites and never merges. A store restored beside somebody else's files is a
+/// store with orphans the first open will delete, which is `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s business and
+/// not this call's.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::NoManifest`] when `source` holds no copy manifest at all, [`Error::Occupied`] when
+/// `destination` already holds a store, [`Error::Damaged`] when the copy is not what its manifest
+/// says it is (a file missing, short, hashing to another checksum, unreadable, or named as a path no
+/// store could hold) — and [`Error::Store`] when the filesystem refuses.
+pub fn restore(source: &Path, destination: &Path) -> Result<Outcome, Error> {
+    let _ = (source, destination);
+    todo!(
+        "lakelet/4.19-backup-restore: load the copy's manifest, refuse a destination that already \
+         holds a store, verify every entry before writing anything, and then write the files — the \
+         store's own manifest last, because that file is the commit point that makes a directory a \
+         store"
+    )
+}
