@@ -0,0 +1,695 @@
+//! The drill `lakelet/4.19-backup-restore` is judged by: a store, a machine death, a copy, and a
+//! restore that has to agree with the store it came from.
+//!
+//! Course content — the step's scaffold ships it, `slow:sim@N` runs it once per seed with
+//! `KILN_SIM_SEED` set, and the gate compares the `lakelet-sim` lines below in the order they were
+//! printed. Three things are therefore strict:
+//!
+//! * **the seed decides the drill.** How many batches the workload writes, how many records each
+//!   carries, which series they are of, after which batch the write path waits for the device, which
+//!   batches a flush closes into a segment, and *where the copy is interrupted* all come out of
+//!   [`KILN_SIM_SEED`](SEED) through the store's own generator (`src/engine/rng.rs`), so the same
+//!   seed walks the same path on every machine and a `replay` of it is byte-identical;
+//! * **the model is the machine death `tests/sim_wal.rs` uses.** That scenario models a machine that
+//!   stopped after a write and before a flush by cutting the log back to the store's own
+//!   `durable_offset` (`lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr`), and this drill borrows exactly that: the bytes
+//!   above the last offset the device was told about are gone with the page cache, and everything
+//!   below it is there. What it does *not* model is a torn or rewritten page — the other three deaths
+//!   of that step — because a copy of a store dead that way is a copy of a shorter log, which is what
+//!   a copy of the drill below already is;
+//! * **the invariants are asserted here, not by the gate.** The gate can only see whether a run
+//!   passed. What this step claims — a complete copy loses nothing and restores to a store that
+//!   answers what its source answered, an interrupted copy loses exactly what its own manifest does
+//!   not name and invents nothing, and the crash's loss is the write path's own policy — is checked
+//!   inside the drill, and a run where one of them is false fails with the sentence that says which.
+//!
+//! # Why the seed is not in a transcript that agreed
+//!
+//! `slow:sim@N` demands that at least two seeds produce *different* transcripts — three identical
+//! ones would mean the drill never read the seed, so one run would have been reported three times. A
+//! transcript that printed its own seed would satisfy that demand without drilling anything, which is
+//! why no line of a run that agreed carries it (`tests/sim_model.rs` makes the same argument for the
+//! walk it ships). A *failing* run is the exception: the panic names the seed, because a reader of a
+//! red log needs the run it came from.
+//!
+//! # What the transcript holds
+//!
+//! One line per observation — the store the drill built, the death it modelled, what the copy took,
+//! what the copy's own manifest says, what the restore wrote, and the comparison at the end — and
+//! every line carries counts and digests. Nothing here prints a path, an address, a wall clock or an
+//! unmasked float: the values in the store are hashed by their bits, so the same seed prints the same
+//! bytes in any process on any machine.
+//!
+//! # What the drill does with the two numbers
+//!
+//! RPO is *measured*: how many bytes and how many records the machine death took off the end of the
+//! log (`written - durable`, and the records those bytes carried), which is the write path's own
+//! policy answering rather than this file asserting. RTO is not measured here at all: a wall clock in
+//! a `slow:sim@N` transcript would make the same seed print two different bytes on a loaded machine,
+//! so the one timing line in this step lives in `lakeletq drill`, behind `LAKELET_DRILL_TIMED`, and
+//! `docs/dr-drill.md` is where it is written down with the machine it came from.
+
+mod support;
+
+use std::path::Path;
+
+use lakelet::engine::Spool;
+use lakelet::engine::backup::{self, Interruption};
+use lakelet::engine::compaction::{self, Segments};
+use lakelet::engine::rng::Rng;
+use lakelet::engine::spool::FILE as LOG;
+use lakelet::engine::wal::Policy;
+use lakelet::engine::wal::replay;
+use lakelet::protocol::batch::{self, Record};
+
+use support::StoreDir;
+
+/// The prefix `slow:sim@N` reads: `<track>-sim`, and the gate ignores every line that is not one.
+const PREFIX: &str = "lakelet-sim";
+
+/// The seed this run uses when the judge does not name one.
+///
+/// Seed `1` is also what `sim/4.19-backup-restore.toml` marks as `replay`, so a learner running this
+/// file by hand sees the drill the gate replays.
+const DEFAULT_SEED: u64 = 1;
+
+/// The variable `slow:sim@N` sets: which drill this run takes.
+const SEED: &str = "KILN_SIM_SEED";
+
+/// The series a drill's workload spreads over.
+///
+/// Three, and not one: a copy that lost a *file* and a copy that lost a *record* have to be
+/// distinguishable in the transcript, and with a single series every count in it would be the same
+/// number under a different name.
+const SERIES: [&str; 3] = ["cpu,host=h0", "cpu,host=h1", "cpu,host=h2"];
+
+/// The earliest instant a drill's records may be stamped with: 2023-11-14T22:13:20Z, a round number
+/// rather than a meaningful date, because nothing in this drill is about what day it was.
+const BASE: i64 = 1_700_000_000_000;
+
+/// How far apart two records of one batch are, in milliseconds.
+const STEP: i64 = 10_000;
+
+/// How far apart two batches are, in milliseconds: the clock moves once per turn of the load, and the
+/// records inside a batch arrive seconds apart.
+const TURN: i64 = 60_000;
+
+/// The seed this process was asked for.
+fn seed() -> u64 {
+    match std::env::var(SEED) {
+        Ok(text) => text
+            .parse()
+            .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("{SEED}=`{text}` is not a number: {error}")),
+        Err(_) => DEFAULT_SEED,
+    }
+}
+
+/// The workload one drill writes, and where the two deaths are put in it.
+///
+/// Everything in here comes out of [`Rng`], which is the store's own generator
+/// (`lakelet/4.13-model-based-tests`): one number in, one drill out, on every machine.
+struct Script {
+    /// The records of every batch, in the order they are appended.
+    batches: Vec<Vec<Record>>,
+    /// After which batch the write path waits for the device: the durable boundary the machine death
+    /// cuts the log back to. One-based, because "after the second batch" is what a reader counts.
+    sync_after: usize,
+    /// How many batches a flush closes into a segment.
+    sealed: usize,
+    /// Where this run's copy stops, or `None` for a copy of the whole store.
+    ///
+    /// The pair is a *slot* and a *percentage*, because the file list a copy walks is not known until
+    /// the store exists: the slot picks which of the files after the store's own manifest to stop
+    /// inside, and the percentage says how far into it to get. A copy of the whole store is still
+    /// asked for as a *budget* rather than as [`Interruption::None`], so both ways of not being
+    /// interrupted are exercised — that one here, and [`Interruption::None`] in the step's spec.
+    interruption: Option<(u64, u64)>,
+}
+
+impl Script {
+    /// The drill `seed` decides.
+    fn drawn(seed: u64) -> Self {
+        let mut rng = Rng::new(seed);
+        let count = rng.between(3, 5) as usize;
+        let mut batches: Vec<Vec<Record>> = Vec::with_capacity(count);
+        for batch in 0..count {
+            let size = rng.between(2, 6) as usize;
+            let mut records: Vec<Record> = Vec::with_capacity(size);
+            for index in 0..size {
+                let series = *rng.pick(&SERIES);
+                let at = BASE + (batch as i64) * TURN + (index as i64) * STEP;
+                records.push(Record {
+                    series: series.to_owned(),
+                    ts_millis: at,
+                    // A value with a fractional part, so a copy that re-encoded a record without its
+                    // value would differ in its bits rather than in a number somebody reads.
+                    value: (rng.below(1_000) as f64) / 8.0,
+                });
+            }
+            batches.push(records);
+        }
+        let sync_after = rng.between(1, count as i64) as usize;
+        let sealed = rng.between(1, count as i64) as usize;
+        // Two runs in five copy the whole store. The rest stop inside one file, and never inside the
+        // *first* one: a copy always names the store's own manifest before anything else, so the
+        // epoch and the floor are stated even when everything after them is missing.
+        let interruption = if rng.chance(40) {
+            None
+        } else {
+            Some((rng.below(1_000), rng.between(1, 99) as u64))
+        };
+        Self {
+            batches,
+            sync_after,
+            sealed,
+            interruption,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// How many records the workload writes.
+    fn records(&self) -> usize {
+        self.batches.iter().map(Vec::len).sum()
+    }
+}
+
+/// One file a copy of `source` takes, in the order the copy takes them: its name in the copy, the
+/// bytes it costs *in the copy*, and where they start.
+///
+/// The first file is the store's own manifest and the last is the log's proven prefix — the order
+/// `lakelet/4.19-backup-restore`'s copy is written in — and the drill needs it to put the
+/// interruption at a seeded point rather than wherever a byte count happened to land.
+struct Taken {
+    /// The file's path relative to the store's root, which is the name its entry carries.
+    name: String,
+    /// How many bytes of it a copy takes: a whole file, or the proven prefix of the log.
+    bytes: u64,
+}
+
+impl Taken {
+    /// The files a copy of `source` takes, in order.
+    fn of(source: &Path, proved: u64) -> Result<Vec<Self>, backup::Error> {
+        let segments = Segments::open(source).map_err(|source| backup::Error::Source { source })?;
+        let mut files = vec![Self {
+            name: compaction::MANIFEST.to_owned(),
+            bytes: length_of(&source.join(compaction::MANIFEST))?,
+        }];
+        for info in segments.manifest().segments() {
+            let path = compaction::file_of(source, info.index);
+            files.push(Self {
+                name: relative(source, &path),
+                bytes: length_of(&path)?,
+            });
+        }
+        let log = source.join(LOG);
+        if log.is_file() {
+            files.push(Self {
+                name: LOG.to_owned(),
+                bytes: proved,
+            });
+        }
+        Ok(files)
+    }
+
+    /// Where a copy stopped, as the interruption that stops it there.
+    ///
+    /// The `slot` picks a file — never the first, because a copy always names the store's own manifest
+    /// before anything else — and the `percent` says how far into it to get: the budget is everything
+    /// before that file plus that share of it, so the copy names the files before it and leaves the
+    /// one it was writing partial and unnamed. A slot that lands past the last file is a budget no
+    /// file reaches, which is a copy of the whole store.
+    fn interrupted_at(files: &[Self], slot: u64, percent: u64) -> Interruption {
+        let after_manifest = files.len().saturating_sub(1).max(1) as u64;
+        let index = 1 + (slot % after_manifest) as usize;
+        let before: u64 = files
+            .iter()
+            .take(index)
+            .fold(0u64, |total, file| total.saturating_add(file.bytes));
+        let Some(file) = files.get(index) else {
+            return Interruption::AfterBytes(before);
+        };
+        let share = file.bytes.saturating_mul(percent.min(99)) / 100;
+        let room = share.clamp(1, file.bytes.saturating_sub(1).max(1));
+        Interruption::AfterBytes(before.saturating_add(room))
+    }
+
+    /// The bytes all of them cost: the budget a copy of the whole store is asked for.
+    fn total(files: &[Self]) -> u64 {
+        files
+            .iter()
+            .fold(0u64, |total, file| total.saturating_add(file.bytes))
+    }
+}
+
+/// The digest of a record list: the series, the timestamp and the value's bits, folded together.
+///
+/// A digest and not a count, because the two answer different questions: a copy that lost one record
+/// and duplicated another keeps the count, and the pair of digests is what says the two stores answer
+/// the same thing. The value travels as its bits (`f64::to_bits`), so no formatter's idea of a float
+/// can make two machines disagree about one store.
+fn digest(records: &[Record]) -> String {
+    let mut hash: u64 = 0xcbf2_9ce4_8422_2325;
+    for record in records {
+        for byte in record.series.as_bytes() {
+            hash = fold(hash, *byte);
+        }
+        hash = fold(hash, 0xff);
+        for byte in record.ts_millis.to_le_bytes() {
+            hash = fold(hash, byte);
+        }
+        for byte in record.value.to_bits().to_le_bytes() {
+            hash = fold(hash, byte);
+        }
+    }
+    format!("{hash:016x}")
+}
+
+/// FNV-1a's step, which is the whole hash: xor one byte in, multiply by the prime.
+fn fold(hash: u64, byte: u8) -> u64 {
+    (hash ^ u64::from(byte)).wrapping_mul(0x0000_0100_0000_01b3)
+}
+
+/// A file's length, or a failure naming it.
+fn length_of(path: &Path) -> Result<u64, backup::Error> {
+    std::fs::metadata(path)
+        .map(|metadata| metadata.len())
+        .map_err(|source| backup::Error::Store {
+            path: path.to_path_buf(),
+            source,
+        })
+}
+
+/// A path as an entry names it: relative to the store's root, `/`-separated.
+fn relative(root: &Path, path: &Path) -> String {
+    path.strip_prefix(root)
+        .unwrap_or(path)
+        .to_string_lossy()
+        .replace('\\', "/")
+}
+
+/// Every record a store's segments hold, in the manifest's order, or `None` when this directory is
+/// not a store this build can open at all.
+///
+/// `None` is not a failure here: an interrupted copy restores to a directory whose manifest names a
+/// segment nobody kept, and a store that refused to open *that* is the answer the drill asserts.
+fn answered(store: &Path) -> Option<Vec<Record>> {
+    let segments = Segments::open(store).ok()?;
+    segments.snapshot().records().ok()
+}
+
+/// The same, for a store the drill already knows opens.
+fn answers(store: &Path) -> Vec<Record> {
+    answered(store).unwrap_or_else(|| {
+        panic!(
+            "{} is not a store this build can open, and this part of the drill is about a store that \
+             opens",
+            store.display()
+        )
+    })
+}
+
+/// The records one segment file holds, by name.
+fn segment_records(store: &Path, name: &str) -> Vec<Record> {
+    lakelet::engine::segment::read(&store.join(name)).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
+        panic!(
+            "the segment {name} of {} could not be read: {error}",
+            store.display()
+        )
+    })
+}
+
+/// The records a log proves, as the payloads' own records.
+fn log_records(store: &Path) -> Vec<Record> {
+    let recovered = replay::recover(store)
+        .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("{}'s log could not be replayed: {error}", store.display()));
+    let mut records = Vec::new();
+    for payload in recovered.records() {
+        records.extend(batch::decode(payload).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
+            panic!(
+                "{}'s log holds something that is not a batch: {error}",
+                store.display()
+            )
+        }));
+    }
+    records
+}
+
+#[test]
+fn a_copy_is_a_backup_only_once_it_has_been_restored() {
+    let seed = seed();
+    let script = Script::drawn(seed);
+    let source = StoreDir::new("sim-backup-source");
+    let copied = StoreDir::new("sim-backup-copy");
+    let restored = StoreDir::new("sim-backup-restored");
+
+    // 1. The store, through the real write path: `Spool` appends what a load would have sent, and a
+    //    scripted flush closes what the batch held into a segment. Nothing waits for the device
+    //    (`Policy::Never`) — the drill decides that, so what the machine death costs is a number of
+    //    records rather than a number of milliseconds on the machine running this.
+    let mut spool = Spool::with_policy(source.path(), Policy::Never)
+        .expect("a directory this process owns is a store");
+    let mut segments =
+        Segments::open(source.path()).expect("the same directory is a segment store");
+    for (index, records) in script.batches.iter().enumerate() {
+        spool
+            .append(&batch::encode(records))
+            .expect("the write path takes what a client sent");
+        if index < script.sealed {
+            segments
+                .seal(records)
+                .expect("a batch a flush wrote is a segment");
+        }
+        if index + 1 == script.sync_after {
+            spool
+                .sync()
+                .expect("the device takes what the policy asks it to");
+        }
+    }
+    let written = spool.len();
+    let durable = spool.durable_offset();
+    let written_records = script.records() as u64;
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} store batches={} records={written_records} segments={} sync_after={} sealed={}",
+        script.batches.len(),
+        segments.manifest().segments().len(),
+        script.sync_after,
+        script.sealed
+    );
+
+    // 2. The machine death: everything above the last offset the device was told about goes with the
+    //    page cache, and the store that comes back is the one an open of this store finds.
+    drop(spool);
+    replay::truncate(source.path(), durable).expect("the log can be cut where the policy promised");
+    let proved = replay::recover(source.path()).expect("what the bytes prove");
+    let proved_records: u64 = proved
+        .records()
+        .iter()
+        .map(|payload| batch::decode(payload).map_or(0, |records| records.len() as u64))
+        .sum();
+    let walked = Spool::open(source.path()).expect("the store a machine came back with");
+    assert_eq!(
+        walked.len(),
+        proved.bytes(),
+        "the store came back with {} byte(s) of log where the replay proved {}: opening a store \
+         continues where the bytes it could prove end, and nowhere else",
+        walked.len(),
+        proved.bytes()
+    );
+    drop(walked);
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} crash written={written} durable={durable} lost_bytes={} lost_records={} stop={}",
+        written.saturating_sub(durable),
+        written_records.saturating_sub(proved_records),
+        proved.stop().name()
+    );
+
+    // 3. The copy, interrupted where the seed says. The budget is the seed's decision about *which*
+    //    file to stop inside and how far in; a seed that drew no interruption asks for the whole
+    //    store, which is the same byte count a copy of everything needs.
+    let files = Taken::of(source.path(), proved.bytes()).expect("the store's file list");
+    let interruption = match script.interruption {
+        Some((slot, percent)) => Taken::interrupted_at(&files, slot, percent),
+        None => Interruption::AfterBytes(Taken::total(&files)),
+    };
+    let report = backup::copy(source.path(), copied.path(), interruption)
+        .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("the store could not be copied: {error}"));
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} copy files={} entries={} bytes={} records={} log_bytes={} log_records={} \
+         interrupted={} expected={}",
+        report.files(),
+        report.entries(),
+        report.bytes(),
+        report.records(),
+        report.log_bytes(),
+        report.log_records(),
+        report.interrupted(),
+        Taken::total(&files)
+    );
+
+    // 4. What the copy's own manifest says about the same copy: the report is the copy's account of
+    //    itself, and the manifest is what a restore will read. They have to be the same copy.
+    let loaded = backup::Manifest::load(copied.path())
+        .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("the copy has no manifest this build can read: {error}"));
+    let named: Vec<&str> = loaded.entries().iter().map(|entry| entry.name()).collect();
+    let named_segments: u64 = loaded
+        .entries()
+        .iter()
+        .filter(|entry| entry.kind() == backup::Kind::Segment)
+        .map(|entry| entry.records().unwrap_or_default())
+        .fold(0u64, |total, records| total.saturating_add(records));
+    let named_log: u64 = loaded
+        .entries()
+        .iter()
+        .filter(|entry| entry.kind() == backup::Kind::Log)
+        .map(|entry| entry.records().unwrap_or_default())
+        .fold(0u64, |total, records| total.saturating_add(records));
+    assert_eq!(
+        loaded.len() as u64,
+        report.entries(),
+        "the copy reports {} entry(ies) and its own manifest holds {}: the report is what the copy \
+         did and the manifest is what the copy says it did, and a restore reads the second",
+        report.entries(),
+        loaded.len()
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        named_segments,
+        report.records(),
+        "the copy reports {} record(s) for the segments it named and its manifest states {named_segments}",
+        report.records()
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        named_log,
+        report.log_records(),
+        "the copy reports {} record(s) for the log and its manifest states {named_log}",
+        report.log_records()
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        loaded.discarded(),
+        0,
+        "a copy this process wrote left {} byte(s) of its own manifest unproved: the entry after \
+         the last complete file was never finished, so the copy lied about what it wrote",
+        loaded.discarded()
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        loaded.epoch(),
+        segments.manifest().epoch(),
+        "the copy's manifest states epoch {} and the store it copied is at epoch {}",
+        loaded.epoch(),
+        segments.manifest().epoch()
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        loaded.floor(),
+        segments.manifest().floor(),
+        "the copy's manifest states floor {} and the store it copied is at floor {}",
+        loaded.floor(),
+        segments.manifest().floor()
+    );
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} manifest entries={} discarded={} stop={} epoch={} floor={} names={}",
+        loaded.len(),
+        loaded.discarded(),
+        loaded.stop().name(),
+        loaded.epoch(),
+        loaded.floor(),
+        named.join(",")
+    );
+
+    // 5. The restore, and the comparison the step is about. A *complete* copy restores to a store
+    //    that answers what its source answered and loses nothing; an interrupted one restores to
+    //    exactly the files its manifest named, and to a store missing what they no longer hold.
+    let outcome = backup::restore(copied.path(), restored.path())
+        .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("the copy could not be restored: {error}"));
+    let source_records = answers(source.path());
+    let source_log = log_records(source.path());
+    let complete = named.len() == files.len();
+    assert_eq!(
+        report.interrupted(),
+        !complete,
+        "the copy names {} of the store's {} file(s) and reports interrupted={}: a copy is \
+         interrupted exactly when it did not take every file",
+        named.len(),
+        files.len(),
+        report.interrupted()
+    );
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} restored files={} bytes={} records={} log_records={} epoch={} floor={}",
+        outcome.files(),
+        outcome.bytes(),
+        outcome.records(),
+        outcome.log_records(),
+        outcome.epoch(),
+        outcome.floor()
+    );
+
+    // The restored files are the copy's entries: same names, same lengths, and no file the manifest
+    // did not cover. This holds for an interrupted copy too, which is the point — a restore writes
+    // what the manifest named, and the manifest is what an interrupted copy narrowed.
+    let mut restored_files: Vec<(String, u64)> = Vec::new();
+    for entry in loaded.entries() {
+        let path = restored.path().join(entry.name());
+        let length = length_of(&path).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
+            panic!(
+                "the restored store has no {}: a restore writes every file the manifest named — {error}",
+                entry.name()
+            )
+        });
+        assert_eq!(
+            length,
+            entry.bytes(),
+            "the restored {} is {length} byte(s) and the manifest states {}",
+            entry.name(),
+            entry.bytes()
+        );
+        restored_files.push((entry.name().to_owned(), length));
+    }
+    let mut on_disk: Vec<(String, u64)> = Vec::new();
+    for entry in std::fs::read_dir(restored.path()).expect("the restored directory can be listed") {
+        let path = entry.expect("a directory entry").path();
+        if path.is_dir() {
+            for inner in std::fs::read_dir(&path).expect("a directory of the restored store") {
+                let inner = inner.expect("a directory entry").path();
+                on_disk.push((
+                    relative(restored.path(), &inner),
+                    length_of(&inner).expect("a file the restore wrote"),
+                ));
+            }
+            continue;
+        }
+        on_disk.push((
+            relative(restored.path(), &path),
+            length_of(&path).expect("a file the restore wrote"),
+        ));
+    }
+    on_disk.sort();
+    restored_files.sort();
+    assert_eq!(
+        on_disk, restored_files,
+        "the restored directory holds {on_disk:?} and the copy's manifest named {restored_files:?}: \
+         a restore writes the files the manifest covered and invents none"
+    );
+
+    // What an interrupted copy loses: the records of the files it does not name, and no others. Every
+    // file it *does* name is compared with the source's own file, which is what "invents nothing"
+    // means — a copy that wrote a segment the source does not have would still pass a count.
+    let mut missing_segments: u64 = 0;
+    for file in &files {
+        if file.name == compaction::MANIFEST || file.name == LOG {
+            continue;
+        }
+        if !named.contains(&file.name.as_str()) {
+            missing_segments = missing_segments
+                .saturating_add(segment_records(source.path(), &file.name).len() as u64);
+            continue;
+        }
+        let copied = segment_records(restored.path(), &file.name);
+        let original = segment_records(source.path(), &file.name);
+        assert_eq!(
+            copied,
+            original,
+            "the restored {} holds {} record(s) and the source's holds {}: a copy of a file is that \
+             file, and a restore is a copy",
+            file.name,
+            copied.len(),
+            original.len()
+        );
+    }
+    assert_eq!(
+        missing_segments,
+        (source_records.len() as u64).saturating_sub(outcome.records()),
+        "the copy does not name {missing_segments} record(s) and the restored store states {} of the \
+         source's {}: the loss is exactly what the manifest does not name, and nothing else",
+        outcome.records(),
+        source_records.len()
+    );
+    let kept_log = log_records(restored.path());
+    let missing_log = (source_log.len() as u64).saturating_sub(kept_log.len() as u64);
+    if named.contains(&LOG) {
+        assert_eq!(
+            kept_log,
+            source_log,
+            "the restored log holds {} record(s) and the source's holds {}: a complete copy takes \
+             the whole prefix a replay proves",
+            kept_log.len(),
+            source_log.len()
+        );
+    } else {
+        assert_eq!(
+            kept_log,
+            source_log[..kept_log.len()],
+            "the restored log holds {} record(s) that are not the source's first {}: a copy takes a \
+             prefix of the log and never a record from the middle of it",
+            kept_log.len(),
+            kept_log.len()
+        );
+    }
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} loss missing_records={} missing_log_records={missing_log} restored_records={} \
+         source_records={}",
+        missing_segments.saturating_add(missing_log),
+        outcome.records(),
+        source_records.len()
+    );
+
+    // And the restored store itself: a complete copy answers what its source answered, record for
+    // record, and an interrupted one does not open as a store at all when a segment is missing.
+    let held = answered(restored.path());
+    if complete {
+        let after = held.clone().unwrap_or_else(|| {
+            panic!(
+                "{} is a store restored from a complete copy and it does not open: every file the \
+                 copy named is here, so what the copy took is not what a store is made of",
+                restored.path().display()
+            )
+        });
+        assert_eq!(
+            digest(&after),
+            digest(&source_records),
+            "the restored store answers {} record(s) and the store it was copied from answered {}: a \
+             complete copy loses nothing, and the digest over the ordered records is what says the \
+             two are the same store rather than two stores with one count",
+            after.len(),
+            source_records.len()
+        );
+        assert_eq!(
+            kept_log,
+            source_log,
+            "the restored store's log proves {} record(s) and the source's proves {}",
+            kept_log.len(),
+            source_log.len()
+        );
+        assert_eq!(
+            after.len() as u64,
+            outcome.records(),
+            "the restored store holds {} record(s) and the restore wrote {}: what a restore reports \
+             is what a store answers with",
+            after.len(),
+            outcome.records()
+        );
+    } else {
+        let segments_named = files
+            .iter()
+            .filter(|file| file.name != compaction::MANIFEST && file.name != LOG)
+            .all(|file| named.contains(&file.name.as_str()));
+        if !segments_named {
+            assert!(
+                held.is_none(),
+                "a store opened {} — a directory restored from a copy that was missing a segment: \
+                 the manifest is the store, so a store whose manifest names a file nobody kept must \
+                 refuse rather than answer with a hole in it",
+                restored.path().display()
+            );
+        }
+    }
+    let after = match &held {
+        Some(records) => digest(records),
+        None => "not-a-store".to_owned(),
+    };
+    println!(
+        "{PREFIX} compared source={} restored={after} epoch={} floor={} log_records={} complete={} \
+         ok=true",
+        digest(&source_records),
+        outcome.epoch(),
+        outcome.floor(),
+        kept_log.len(),
+        complete
+    );
+}
